\h Tsefanyah
\toc1 Tsefanyah
\toc2 Tsefanyah
\toc3 Tsef.
\mt1 Tsefanyah
\mt2 (Zephaniah)
\is1 Introduction
\ip Tsefanyah (commonly known as Zephaniah) was a prophet at the time that Yoshiyah (Josiah) was king of Yehudah (Judah). The people served the true God, but before long, they abandoned Yahweh and started worshipping false gods. So then Yahweh threatened to punish the people of Yerushalem (Jerusalem) city and the people across all of Yehudah because of their evil activities. However, even then, they refused to ask him for forgiveness. So in 586 before Yeshua/Jesus (B.C.), Yerushalem was destroyed by the Babylonians (sometimes also called Chaldeans). But even then, he'd planned to punish those other nations in the future for enslaving his people, then he'd allow his people to return there to Yerushalem and receive his blessing.
\iot Main components of this document
\io1 The day of Yahweh's judgement \ior 1:1–2:3\ior*
\io1 The destruction of nations near Yisrael \ior 2:4-15\ior*
\io1 The destruction and future redemption of Yerushalem \ior 3:1-20\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
